y and a server socket IS a durable write, so the landed no-replace + fsync + hooks + real-kill pattern applies, or state an explicit purity bound; a conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test or a stated bound; a README package section with test commands and NO capability or CLI claim (no `ax` command exists yet); a LOGBOOK entry, newest-first.\n19|\n20|WORKSPACE. This is the FIRST leaf, so the managed Story worktree does not exist yet — the spawn provisions `.temp/STORY-260830-2t4g7i/worktree` on branch `task-board/story/STORY-260830-2t4g7i` forked from trunk `799c338`. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree there: exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-35urbp`, then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts. Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths.\n21|\n22|PROCESS. Read the Curator-managed `go-testing-tools` skill first. All CI is local; never trigger hosted CI. Run your own package tests and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured validation suite once — read the command count from the candidate's `task-board.config.json` (it is 30 at trunk 799c338, not 27); command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs, which is expected and must not be shortened. Report the coverage ratio you MEASURED. Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in; if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-35urbp_results.md, TASK-260830-35urbp_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-35urbp_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-35urbp --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n23|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n24|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n25|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n26|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n27|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n28|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n29|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n30|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3e9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c52f-2ee9-7e90-86a5-5469e2a72bf6"},"sequence":79,"recorded_at":1780531400000153,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"0c8ad2d8-68e1-4b10-83fc-51c6169a80fc","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"0c8ad2d8-68e1-4b10-83fc-51c6169a80fc","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"0c8ad2d8-68e1-4b10-83fc-51c6169a80fc"},"call_id":"call_01a0c5348c1b7562b3f75c6119c25c81","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-35urbp/TASK-260830-35urbp_rework-rev6.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-35urbp_rework-rev5.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-35urbp, the FIRST of three leaves of STORY-260830-2t4g7i. Revision 5 was reviewed by RUN-260921-60d15f (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED with **no P1**: one P2 (four measured members) and two P3. Read TASK-260830-35urbp_review-verdict-rev5.md first. Every rev4 finding — the oracle fix and the admit-direction narrowing, `Request.Name`, the platform axis, the spy census, the five observation sentences — is closed with driven evidence and stays closed. **Production behaves correctly at every point five consecutive reviews have driven.** Keep all of it.\n3|Trunk has NOT moved (base is still 799c338). Your working tree is the rev5 candidate; build revision 6 on it. Do not refresh, do not rebase.\n4|\n5|**READ THIS BEFORE THE ITEM LIST, BECAUSE IT IS THE ACTUAL TASK.** Three rounds in a row have produced the same class of finding at a new site each time: rev3 P2-C (the running-server rule pinned at the entry only for servers carrying admission data), rev4 P2-B (`Request.Name` free at the entry while the path was pinned at the constants), and now rev5 P2-A (the broker-or-refuse rule pinned at the entry only for the shapes the fixtures happen to build). Each round I handed you the specific tests the reviewer had already measured, you closed exactly those, and the next review found the same class one site over. **That means the defect is your measurement, not your code.** So this round the deliverable is the instrument, and the enumerated items below are only its first outputs.\n6|\n7|Build and commit a **gate × entry census**. For every gate in the package, list every production entry that can reach it — `Acquire` foreground, `Acquire` background, the exported `EnsureRuntimeDir`, `VerifyRuntimeDir`, `BuildArgv`, `SocketPath`, `CheckBrokerContact`, `CheckServerAttested` and anything else that composes them. For every (gate, entry) cell, state one of exactly three things: a named committed test that drives the gate THROUGH that entry and an admitting narrowing row that it kills; or `unreachable` with the reason a caller cannot get there; or a stated BOUND naming what is not measured and who owns it. Put the census in the conformance matrix as its own table and mirror its shape in TRACEABILITY. A cell that says \"pinned at the helper\" and nothing else is exactly the hole the last three reviews walked through — a helper pin plus an entry that reaches the same gate is two cells, not one. Count the cells and report the ratio you MEASURE. If the census is honest and some cells are bounds, that is an acceptable revision; another round of four tests without the census is not.\n8|\n9|The four members the reviewer already measured are your first four cells, and the probes named are templates:\n10|1. **Broker-side attested-server wiring has no admitting narrowing.** SPEC §4.2 1419-1424 requires the background caller to contact an already-running authenticated broker **and its attested server**, else `capability_unavailable`, never a fallback. Four plants that each admit one realistic member SURVIVED both passes: the zero `RealmAdmission{}` report with a live same-user generation-bound principal (the nothing-reconciled state, the exact shape rev3 closed on the FOREGROUND path); rows present with an empty generation against a bound wanted generation; a decoy-only admission with a bound generation (the member the foreground entry already pins); and a background miss whose `Dependencies` carry the foreground `ProbeServer`, which re-enters foreground creation — realistic, because the lifecycle adapter will pass one shared `Dependencies` value to every caller, and no committed background test arms `ProbeServer`, so the `Spawn`-keyed spy census never sees it. Ship: three subtests on `TestAcquireBackgroundMissReturnsTypedUnavailable` asserting the literal `capability_unavailable`, exit 6, the five typed details and zero spawns; the same three rows mirrored on `TestCheckBrokerContactRefusesEachMissingFact`; a narrowing row at the `CheckBrokerContact` server wiring named under rows 27/28/36; a fail-if-called `ProbeServer` spy armed in every background miss test plus `TestAcquireBackgroundVerifiesWithoutCreating`, shipped as a second labelled additive row `D-background-fallback-foreground`; and the row-31/README sentence extended to say the census arms BOTH foreground dependencies.\n11|2. **The absence classifier's reject class is pinned at the entry for mode and containment only.** Widening `runtimeAbsent` to admit `runtime ownership` SURVIVED both passes: no committed background `Acquire` test stages foreign ownership, so a foreign-owned leaf on the background path would return `capability_unavailable` with the custody error redacted as its cause — refusal-to-refusal, but row 52 says an unsafe leaf keeps its custody code as the TOP-LEVEL `*Error`, never the wrapper. Add `TestAcquireBackgroundStagesOwnershipRefusal` (the `effectiveUID` seam is the lever) under row 52, or state the ownership member as helper-pinned only in the census.\n12|3. **A censusable \"before\" that is false.** `acquire.go:140-143` and `results.md:61-62` both claim malformed input, ambient reuse and missing dependencies \"refuse before any side effect\". A foreground request with `SessionID = \"12345\"` **creates `<root>/tmux` and calls `ProbeServer` before** refusing `tmux_invalid_arguments at session identity`: the session identity is validated only inside `BuildArgv`, after `EnsureRuntimeDir` and the probe. Either parse the session at the `Acquire` entry before any side effect and add no-directory/no-probe assertions to `TestAcquireForegroundRefusesMalformedSession` (row 45), or reword both sentences to the members that are actually true. Rejection pattern (f), and the third time a sentence with a universal quantifier has cost a round — before you write \"every\", \"all\", \"never\" or \"before any\", make the census cell that proves it.\n13|4. **Three observation sentences for TRACEABILITY**, all cheap: a comma inside the runtime root makes `tmuxEnvSocket` split at the wrong comma so a real-encoding `TMUX` value walks past the collision gate — tmux(1) itself splits at the first comma, so the decoding is faithful and the honest text is \"roots containing a comma are outside the TMUX member's encoding\" in row 23/B16; a cwd-relative spelling of the derived socket walks past the cleaned-string collision gate, the same bound class as the symlink alias, so B16 should read \"symlinked or relative alias\"; and `Dependencies.Spawn` is inert on the background path.\n14|\n15|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Run your own package tests, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...` (keep it green), and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once, reading the commands from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands` in the candidate config. A second Story is producing concurrently on this host, so command 5 will be slow; do NOT shorten it, and if a gate times out report the measured elapsed time and the host load rather than arguing it away. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table AND the gate × entry census with its measured ratio, update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive (a real gzip archive, MANIFEST with no self-entry, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, zero `__pycache__`), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-35urbp --role developer` so revision 6 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 6.\n16|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n17|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n18|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n19|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n20|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n21|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n22|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n23|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
